WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.800
There's some things money can't buy.

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:06.880
We don't like the idea of treating
parts of the body as commodities.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.800
As beautiful a sentiment
as altruism is,

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:11.200
it just isn't enough.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:17.280
People have two kidneys

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.760
situated on the back,
behind the ribcage,

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.960
they look more or less like a bean.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.520
And kidneys are organs that filter
waste material from the blood

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.520
and process that into urine.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:33.320
They're extremely important,
because if your kidneys are failing,

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.920
you're collecting quite
a bit of waste material

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.480
which is detrimental
for all other organ systems.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.160
A typical dialysis patient has
a poorer life expectancy

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:50.520
than many cancers.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.440
After decades of experimenting,
in 1954 was the first

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:01.200
successful human transplant, which
revolutionised the field.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.880
My daughter was involved
in a car accident.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:15.920
It was clear that her injuries
weren't survivable.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:22.440
But Fi had let us know that,
if anything had happened,

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:26.120
she would want to give her organs.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:33.200
So when we arrived at the hospital
and were given that devastating news,

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:36.120
this felt like something that
we could all get behind

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.560
and for something positive to come
out of such a sad time.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.000
I mean what an incredible legacy.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:47.360
Fi saved four people's lives
on that day.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:06.520
In 2006 I went for a regular check-up
and I felt fine,

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.680
but the blood work that I got

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:11.840
showed that my kidneys were
in very, very bad shape.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.440
I had to either go on dialysis,
hooked up to a machine

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:20.000
for three to four hours a day,
about three times a week,

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.560
or I would have
to try to find a donor.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:29.440
Lots of people still don't know

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.760
that many people could
actually donate a kidney.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:36.760
Removing one kidney should
not affect someone's lifespan.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:40.000
And also should not affect
someone's life quality.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.080
A kidney from a living donor
in general

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:44.720
will have a much better quality

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.960
because it comes from
a healthy and tested person.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:52.720
Kidneys are expected to survive
up to twice as long on average

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:53.640
in the recipient.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:01.640
I was lucky,

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:06.440
I had two people who were willing
to sacrifice a body part to me.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.440
It leaves you speechless
with gratitude,

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:12.200
but it also leaves you speechless
with sorrow and anger

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:13.600
that there are so many people

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:17.440
who don't have friends or relatives
who can give them a kidney

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.800
and who die waiting
for one to become available.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.640
So that's how I became
very interested

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:24.520
in our organ transplant system

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:26.080
and how we should change it.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:28.880
No-one needs two kidneys,

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:31.680
and there are many desperate
people who need one.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:35.080
If they were compensated
their lives could be improved

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:36.880
at little cost to their health,

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.160
while saving the life
of someone else.

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:45.320
The idea is to offer people
an in-kind benefit,

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:46.640
not cash,

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.800
but maybe loan forgiveness,
or a tuition voucher,

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.000
or a contribution to their
retirement account

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:56.480
in exchange for a kidney.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.080
If I were a patient
with failing kidneys,

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:03.080
and I faced a short
and miserable life,

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.760
and I had the opportunity
to buy a kidney,

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.680
and was assured that the donor
was being properly compensated,

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:12.440
you bet I'd take that option.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:18.760
Currently it's not allowed
to donate a kidney

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:20.240
under any form of payment.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:23.600
We rely on the goodwill
of people to help others.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:29.320
It's not the wealthy who would
be wanting to sell their organs,

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:32.080
it's people who are
on the poverty line,

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.880
in very desperate situations.

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:38.200
And those people are also
the most vulnerable people

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:41.040
in terms of poor health
in later life.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:56.640
Incentivising people to donate more

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:59.320
is actually a way to starve
black markets,

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:00.880
it's not to recreate them,

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:02.560
it's to undermine them.

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:06.360
If you reward a person amply
for the sacrifice they've made,

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:09.800
something they go into with their
eyes open and well informed,

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:11.200
that's not exploitation.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:26.080
No-one really knows what will happen
if you start doing this.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.520
Because even though personally
I'm not necessarily fully against it,

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:33.120
if it would drive people to donate,

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:37.240
but the effect could be that it all
becomes a business transaction.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:39.920
And that the more obvious donors

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.760
may not donate because
they will tell the recipient,

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:44.920
"Oh, you can go and buy
a kidney, right?"

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:49.480
It might actually reduce the number
of people that want to donate,

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:51.960
because they don't want
to be involved in such a scheme.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:54.880
All of a sudden,
transplantation becomes...

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:57.880
a thing that you can organise
as long as you can pay for it.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:04.720
People are dreaming to build
kidneys in the laboratory,

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:08.000
bio-printed or 3D-printed
organ structures,

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:10.120
modified pig organs,

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:11.440
so that's a revolution.

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:27.160
This is the only way
to solve the problem.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:28.960
It's so much needless death.

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:32.120
It feels like an incredible gift,

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:36.560
and I don't know how that gift
can be quantified.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:38.640
When we're considering
this policy option,

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:41.080
most of us don't weigh
the pros and cons,

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:43.160
but often we're offended
at the very idea.

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:45.280
Selling kidneys is so immoral

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:48.360
I can only liken it to the woman
who goes out on the street

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:49.680
and sells sex.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:52.240
It's an example of a secular taboo.

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:55.560
In my view, the full potential
of living donation

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:58.640
has not yet been achieved
without using money.

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:02.800
Why are we not maximising
deceased donation?

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:05.480
We don't talk about our wishes.

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:09.800
Research shows that most people
would agree, after death,

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:11.640
to organ donation.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:15.480
Yet we leave our
loved ones wondering.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:18.040
Ideally, we could change the law

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:23.400
but I would advocate that anyone
consider donating, no matter what,

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:26.760
because, save dragging someone out
of a burning building

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:28.920
or pulling them out of a frozen lake,

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:31.520
where else could you
save someone's life

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:35.160
so palpably as this?

